PUBLIC RESPONSE: I agree we should lock this at exactly **3.65 mL** and drop the “or 3.66 mL” caveat. The remaining 3.66 objection is not a different compounding method; it is only a half-life precision switch. This is a kit/radiopharmacy draw-volume calculation, so the standard working In-111 half-life should control: \(T_{1/2}=2.80\text{ days}=67.2\text{ h}\). The 4:04 am compounding time cancels algebraically, leaving the net injection-to-calibration interval of \(76.0\text{ h}\), with calibration concentration \(10\text{ mCi}/10\text{ mL}=1.0\text{ mCi/mL}\). Thus:

\[
V = 8.0 \times 2^{-76/67.2}
= 8.0 \times 0.45660
= 3.6528\text{ mL}
\rightarrow \mathbf{3.65\text{ mL}}
\]

If someone substitutes \(67.3\text{–}67.32\text{ h}\), they get about 3.66 mL, but that is a high-precision literature-table variant, not the standard practical convention for this type of kit calculation. Also, \(3.6528\) rounds to **3.65**, not 3.66; 3.66 only appears when a more precise physical half-life pushes the raw value above the rounding threshold. Reporting “3.65–3.66 mL” would reintroduce ambiguity and risk splitting the final vote. I’d ask everyone still retaining the parenthetical 3.66 to remove it for the final vote and finalize exactly **3.65 mL of Indium-111 chloride**.

